Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=15^\circ$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=30^\circ$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4\,\sec\left(30^\circ\right)}{\sin\left(30^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(30^\circ\right)\cos\left(30^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(60^\circ\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(60^\circ\right)=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{3}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8}{\left(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{16}{\sqrt{3}}\\&=\frac{16 \sqrt{3}}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=67.5^\circ$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=135^\circ$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{4\,\sec\left(135^\circ\right)}{\sin\left(135^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(135^\circ\right)\cos\left(135^\circ\right)}\\&=\frac{8}{\sin\left(270^\circ\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(270^\circ\right)=-1$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{8}{\left(-1\right)}\\&=-8.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{5 \pi}{16}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{5 \pi}{8}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{6\,\sec\left(\frac{5 \pi}{8}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\frac{6}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{8}\right)\cos\left(\frac{5 \pi}{8}\right)}\\&=\frac{12}{\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{4}\right)}
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(\frac{5 \pi}{4}\right)=- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}$.
Rationalize the denominator by multiplying numerator and denominator by $\sqrt{2}$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{12}{\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right)}\\&=\frac{-24}{\sqrt{2}}\\&=- 12 \sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Let $E$ denote the given expression. Apply $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ at $x=\frac{\pi}{24}$ in the denominator. Use $\sec x=1/\cos x$, then apply the double-angle identity again at $x=\frac{\pi}{12}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\frac{2\,\sec\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{2}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{12}\right)}\\&=\frac{4}{\sin\left(\frac{\pi}{6}\right)}
